{"data": [{"author_id": "1873277766", "created_at": "2026-11-03T21:49:12.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117735874905469034"], "id": "2117735874905469034", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 21, "impression_count": 8603, "like_count": 151, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 12, "retweet_count": 6}, "text": "That post made me wonder whether any country in history had a larger within-country fertility difference than Czechoslovakia in 1930. In Prague, the TFR was around 1.0, while it was 4.9 in Ruthenia (present-day Zakarpattia Oblast in Ukraine). Czechoslovakia had a TFR of 2.55. https://t.co/wtABEouAxI", "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2117734304419664076", "type": "quoted"}], "url": "https://x.com/BirthGauge/status/2117735874905469034"}, {"author_id": "1534029770077310976", "created_at": "2026-11-03T22:32:52.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117746861830459790"], "id": "2117746861830459790", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 2, "impression_count": 640, "like_count": 16, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 2, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "People thought Indias tfr will only get to 1.3 by 2050\n\nBut u already see the tfr below 1.9 since 2024 &amp; no signs of stabilization \n\nIndias median age is already ~29 in 2026 so it’s going to happen much sooner especially if tfr continues plummeting https://t.co/M3aOfUm0uX", "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "1985560210011029831", "type": "quoted"}], "url": "https://x.com/McBeaner69/status/2117746861830459790"}, {"author_id": "1873277766", "created_at": "2026-11-03T21:42:58.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117734304419664076"], "id": "2117734304419664076", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 45, "impression_count": 19026, "like_count": 293, "quote_count": 3, "reply_count": 7, "retweet_count": 20}, "text": "95 years of demographic change, Prague edition.\nIn 1930, the Czech capital was dominated by people in their 20s, while WWI had caused a big gap in the 10-15 year olds. The TFR was very low, around 1.0 children per woma